BACKGROUND
====================

The high level summary being internally it uses a direct buffer so buf.put touches the real mmaped page not an intermediate which is good

Also it does mmap(null,....fd) not mmap(malloced_memory,....fd) which on Linux atleast would have created 2x pages

At a high level there are a few drawbacks:
-> cant unmap though for my cache purposes I dont care :-)
   http://bugs.sun.com/bugdatabase/view_bug.do?bug_id=4724038

-> can only map 2G at a time 
   http://bugs.sun.com/bugdatabase/view_bug.do?bug_id=6347833


======================== JAVA CODE ===========================================
public class MemoryMap {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        long FILESIZE = 1024 * 1024 * 1024;
        System.out.println("size is " + FILESIZE);
        System.out.println("about to map file");
        Thread.sleep(5000);
        MappedByteBuffer buf = new RandomAccessFile("/tmp/data.dat", "rw").getChannel().map(MapMode.READ_WRITE, 0,
                                   FILESIZE);
        long counter = 0;

        for (counter = 0; counter < FILESIZE; counter++) {
            buf.put((byte) 2);
        }
        System.out.println("counter value is " + counter);
        buf.force();
        System.out.println("done with flush");
        Thread.sleep(8000);

    }
}

